course: dictionary
lesson: 7621
title: Legal words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are used mostly by grown-ups, and they may be new to you. Good readers use the context, the words around the word, to work out what it means. «Probate is the official permission from a court to deal with a dead person's money and property.» The words dead person, money and property give you the clues.
explain: Look at what happens next in the sentence: «until it comes the house usually cannot be sold.» So probate must be something that has to come before a family can use or sell what was left behind. Ask yourself: what would fit in this place, and does my meaning still make sense?
explain: The sentence also says «it can take months.» That tells you probate is a slow process, not something done in one afternoon. Words like court and permission show that it is a legal matter, something decided by the law.
explain: Probate often comes up when a grandparent dies and the family talks about the will. A will is a written paper that says who should get a person's things. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it, and a grown-up can help you ask the right questions.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Probate» means the official legal process that checks that a dead person's will is real.
fact: Probate lets a dead person's money and belongings be shared out as the will says.
fact: In the USA, «probate» can also be a verb: to probate a will means to prove it in court.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «grant of probate», «go through probate» and «probate court».
exercise: «Probate is the official permission from a court to deal with a dead person's money and property.» What does probate mean? | A family party held after a funeral | A type of house that is for sale | *The official legal process that checks a dead person's will is real, so their things can be shared out | A letter sent to school about homework
exercise: «Until probate comes, the house usually cannot be sold; it can take months.» What does this tell you about probate? | *It can be a slow process that takes a long time | It is a kind of holiday | It has nothing to do with houses | It always takes just one day
exercise: Where might you hear the phrase «probate court»? | In a shop that sells bread and cakes | *In a court that deals with wills and money after someone dies | In a school library during story time | At the top of a mountain
test: In the USA, what does it mean to probate a will? | *To prove the will in court | To write a letter to the bank | To sell the house quickly to a neighbour | To hide the will in a drawer
test: Why can a family usually not sell a house straight after someone dies? | Because it is raining outside | *Because probate has not yet come through | Because the house is too old to sell | Because the children want to keep it forever
exam: Grandma has died, and the family must wait for probate before sharing her belongings. What is probate? | A kind of tasty cake eaten at a funeral | A name for a holiday by the sea | *The official legal process that checks a will is real, so belongings can be shared out | A word for a very large garden
